   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/1Bpp4/3P1B2/4PN2/PPP2PPP/RN1QK2R b KQkq - 3 5",
   "line": [
    "Bb5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop out to b5 is slightly offbeat — normally the c-pawn goes first in the London. And here's a deep positional idea: a queen check to a5 doesn't win the bishop, but at this level you play moves that induce a move you want. The whole London rests on d4 being a fortress, and it's rock-solid only once c3 supports it.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 131.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/qBpp4/3P1B2/4PN2/PPP2PPP/RN1QK2R w KQkq - 4 6",
   "line": [
    "Qa5+"
   ],
   "spoken": "So the check induces a knight to c3, which permanently denies the c-pawn that support — and d4 flips from a strength into a weakness. As a bonus the queen stands beautifully active, and there's nothing wrong with leaning on the bishop while you're at it.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 142.5,
   "fen": "r1b1kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/qBpp4/3P1B2/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 5 6",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 166.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/qBpp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 6 7",
   "line": [
    "Bg4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Stay active — e6 now would be too passive with development lagging, inviting a central knight leap. The move is a bishop to g4, pinning the knight that props up d4 while developing a piece. Restrain and develop in one stroke.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 172.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/pp2pppp/2B2n2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "Bxc6+"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 173.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p3pppp/2p2n2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "bxc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishops come off, and the recapture toward the centre leaves a compact, healthy pawn structure — the kind of solid shell that's a pleasure to play from."
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 179,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p3pppp/2p2n2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 b kq - 1 8",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 216.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p4ppp/2p1pn2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN2/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "e6"
   ],
   "spoken": "There's a choice of resolving the centre now or playing e6 first — and the guiding rule when unsure is keep the tension. Let the opponent be the one to decide whether to capture; carrying that question is always more of a headache for them than for you.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 221,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p4ppp/2p1pn2/q1pp4/3P1Bb1/2N1PN1P/PPP2PP1/R2Q1RK1 b kq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "h3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 248.5,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p4ppp/2p1pn2/q1pp3b/3P1B2/2N1PN1P/PPP2PP1/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 1 10",
   "line": [
    "Bh5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Take on the knight, or retreat the bishop? The deciding question is whether the opponent would realistically lash out with a weakening kingside pawn push — and the answer is no, there's no reason to fear it. So hold the tension and drop the bishop back.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 255,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p4ppp/2p1pn2/q1pp3b/3P1BP1/2N1PN1P/PPP2P2/R2Q1RK1 b kq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "g4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 271,
   "fen": "r3kb1r/p4ppp/2p1pnb1/q1pp4/3P1BP1/2N1PN1P/PPP2P2/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "Bg6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
